// Domain 3: Deployment, Provisioning, and Automation (22%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d3-01", d: 3,
  q: "A company builds its golden AMI by hand each month. An engineer launches an instance, installs patches, the CloudWatch agent, and security hardening, creates an AMI, and copies it to three AWS Regions. The process takes a day and sometimes misses steps.\n\nWhich solution will automate this process with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an EC2 Image Builder pipeline with a recipe, test components, a monthly schedule, and distribution to the three Regions.",
      "Write a Lambda function that launches an instance, runs a script with Run Command, creates an AMI, and copies it to each Region.",
      "Create an Auto Scaling group whose user data installs everything, and create an AMI from the newest instance each month.",
      "Use AWS CloudFormation to launch an instance each month with cfn-init, and create the AMI by hand after the stack completes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["EC2 Image Builder automates building, testing, and distributing images. A recipe lists the base image and components such as patching, the CloudWatch agent, and hardening. Test components validate the image, a schedule runs the pipeline monthly, and distribution settings copy the AMI to other Regions and accounts.",
      "This works, but it is custom code that must handle failures, testing, and cleanup, which Image Builder already provides.",
      "Creating AMIs from running instances is not repeatable or tested, and running the install in user data adds boot time to every launch.",
      "The AMI step is still manual, and there is no built-in testing or multi-Region distribution."] },

{ id: "d3-02", d: 3,
  q: "An EC2 Image Builder pipeline creates a new AMI every week. After a year, the account has hundreds of old AMIs and snapshots that increase storage costs. The company wants to keep only the 5 newest AMIs and to deprecate older images before they are deleted.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Image Builder lifecycle policy that deprecates and then deletes images beyond the 5 newest.",
      "Create an S3 Lifecycle rule on the bucket that stores the AMIs to expire objects older than 5 weeks.",
      "Turn on Recycle Bin for AMIs so that old images are removed automatically after 5 weeks.",
      "Use AWS Config to mark old AMIs as noncompliant so that EC2 deletes them automatically."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Image Builder lifecycle policies can deprecate, disable, and delete images and their snapshots based on count or age, and they can exclude images that are in use. This keeps the 5 newest AMIs without custom scripts.",
      "AMIs and their snapshots are not stored as objects in a bucket that you manage, so S3 Lifecycle rules do not apply to them.",
      "Recycle Bin keeps deleted AMIs so that they can be recovered. It does not delete old images for you.",
      "Config reports compliance. It does not delete resources unless you build remediation, and EC2 does not delete noncompliant AMIs."] },

{ id: "d3-03", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer shares an AMI with another AWS account. The AMI's snapshots are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key. Users in the other account can see the AMI, but their launches fail with a KMS error.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Update the KMS key policy to allow the other account to use the key, and have that account grant its users access.",
      "Re-encrypt the snapshots with the AWS managed key aws/ebs so that the other account can use the AMI.",
      "Make the AMI public so that any account can launch it without needing access to the KMS key.",
      "Share the AMI with the whole organization instead of sharing it with only the one account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["To launch an AMI with encrypted snapshots, the other account needs permission to use the KMS key, such as kms:Decrypt, kms:DescribeKey, kms:CreateGrant, and kms:ReEncrypt*. The key policy must allow the other account, and that account's IAM policies must allow its users. The other account can also copy the AMI and re-encrypt it with its own key.",
      "Snapshots encrypted with the AWS managed key cannot be shared with other accounts, because the policy of that key cannot be changed.",
      "AMIs with encrypted snapshots cannot be made public, and public images are a security risk.",
      "Sharing with an organization still requires access to the KMS key, so launches would fail in the same way."] },

{ id: "d3-04", d: 3,
  q: "A CI pipeline pushes a new container image to an Amazon ECR repository on every commit. The repository now has thousands of untagged images, and storage costs are rising. The team needs only the last 20 release images and no untagged images older than 7 days.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Add an ECR lifecycle policy that expires untagged images after 7 days and keeps the last 20 release images.",
      "Add a step to the CI pipeline that lists and deletes old images after each push by using the AWS CLI.",
      "Turn on ECR replication to another Region, and delete the source repository once each month.",
      "Turn on enhanced scanning so that Amazon Inspector deletes the old and vulnerable images."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ECR lifecycle policies expire images automatically based on rules such as tag status, tag prefix, image count, and image age. One policy can expire untagged images older than 7 days and keep only the 20 newest images with a release tag prefix.",
      "Custom cleanup in the pipeline is code to maintain, and it runs only when the pipeline runs.",
      "Replication copies images. Deleting the repository each month would destroy images that are still needed.",
      "Enhanced scanning finds vulnerabilities. It does not delete images."] },

{ id: "d3-05", d: 3,
  q: "A security team requires that all container images in Amazon ECR are scanned for vulnerabilities in both operating system packages and programming language packages when they are pushed. Images must also be rescanned automatically when new CVEs are published.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on enhanced scanning for the registry with continuous scanning, which uses Amazon Inspector.",
      "Turn on basic scanning with scan on push for each repository in the registry.",
      "Run a vulnerability scanner in the CI pipeline before each image is pushed to the registry.",
      "Turn on GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for the ECS clusters that run the images."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Enhanced scanning integrates Amazon ECR with Amazon Inspector. It scans images for operating system and programming language package vulnerabilities on push and keeps rescanning them as new vulnerabilities are published.",
      "Basic scanning checks operating system packages when images are pushed or scanned manually. It does not rescan images continuously or cover language packages.",
      "A pipeline scan checks the image once, before it is pushed. It does not rescan stored images when new CVEs appear.",
      "Runtime Monitoring detects threats in running workloads. It does not scan images for known vulnerabilities."] },

{ id: "d3-06", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation template hardcodes Amazon Linux AMI IDs for each Region in a Mappings section. The IDs go out of date, and new stacks launch old, unpatched images.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer make the template always use the latest Amazon Linux AMI?",
  o: ["Use an SSM parameter type that reads the latest AMI ID from the public Systems Manager parameter.",
      "Add a Lambda-backed custom resource that searches for the newest image by name in each Region.",
      "Update the Mappings section by hand each month with the latest AMI IDs for every Region.",
      "Set the ImageId property to latest so that Amazon EC2 picks the newest Amazon Linux image."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS publishes the latest Amazon Linux AMI IDs as public Systems Manager parameters, such as /aws/service/ami-amazon-linux-latest/al2023-ami-kernel-default-x86_64. A template parameter of type AWS::SSM::Parameter::Value<AWS::EC2::Image::Id> resolves the current ID in each Region when the stack is created or updated.",
      "A custom resource works, but it is code to maintain, and the public parameters already provide this data.",
      "Manual updates are what caused the problem. They are slow and easy to forget.",
      "ImageId must be a real AMI ID. Amazon EC2 does not accept latest as a value."] },

{ id: "d3-07", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer needs to update a production CloudFormation stack that contains an Amazon RDS DB instance. Before the update runs, the engineer must confirm that no resource will be replaced.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Create a change set, and review the Replacement value for each resource before executing it.",
      "Run drift detection on the stack, and check whether the database shows a status of MODIFIED.",
      "Update the stack directly with termination protection turned on so that no resource is replaced.",
      "Delete the stack with a Retain deletion policy, and create a new stack from the updated template."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A change set shows how an update will change each resource before anything happens, including whether each resource will be replaced (True, False, or Conditional). The engineer can review it and execute it only if it is safe.",
      "Drift detection compares resources with the current template. It does not preview the changes in a new template.",
      "Termination protection prevents the whole stack from being deleted. It does not stop an update from replacing a resource.",
      "Deleting and re-creating the stack is risky and unnecessary just to preview changes."] },

{ id: "d3-08", d: 3,
  q: "A security audit finds that a security group created by a CloudFormation stack allows SSH from 0.0.0.0/0, but the template does not include that rule. A CloudOps engineer suspects that someone changed the security group in the console.\n\nWhich CloudFormation feature will confirm this and show the difference?",
  o: ["Drift detection on the stack",
      "A change set for the stack",
      "A stack policy on the stack",
      "Termination protection on the stack"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Drift detection compares the current configuration of stack resources with the configuration that the template expects. It reports the security group as MODIFIED and shows the exact property differences, such as the extra ingress rule.",
      "A change set previews the changes from a new template. It does not detect changes made outside CloudFormation.",
      "A stack policy limits which resources stack updates can change. It does not detect or block console changes.",
      "Termination protection prevents the stack from being deleted. It does not detect changes."] },

{ id: "d3-09", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack contains an Amazon RDS DB instance. The company wants a final snapshot to be created if the stack is deleted, and also if a stack update ever replaces the DB instance.\n\nWhich attributes should a CloudOps engineer set on the DB instance resource?",
  o: ["DeletionPolicy: Snapshot and UpdateReplacePolicy: Snapshot",
      "DeletionPolicy: Retain and a stack policy that denies Update:*",
      "DependsOn the stack's S3 bucket and UpdatePolicy: Snapshot",
      "A CreationPolicy with a ResourceSignal and DeletionPolicy: Delete"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DeletionPolicy controls what happens when the resource is removed from the stack or the stack is deleted. UpdateReplacePolicy controls what happens to the old resource when an update replaces it. Snapshot on both creates a final DB snapshot in each case.",
      "Retain keeps the instance instead of taking a snapshot, and denying all updates blocks legitimate changes.",
      "DependsOn controls creation order, and UpdatePolicy applies to resources such as Auto Scaling groups. Neither creates snapshots.",
      "A CreationPolicy waits for signals during creation, and a Delete policy removes the instance without a snapshot."] },

{ id: "d3-10", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack manages a production web tier and a production database. The team updates the web tier often. The company wants stack updates to never modify or replace the database unless someone explicitly overrides the protection.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Apply a stack policy that denies Update:* on the database resource and allows updates on all other resources.",
      "Turn on termination protection for the stack so that updates cannot change the database resource.",
      "Set a DeletionPolicy of Retain on the database so that updates cannot change any of its properties.",
      "Create an IAM policy that denies the cloudformation:UpdateStack action for every user in the account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A stack policy defines which resources stack updates can change. Denying Update:* on the database protects it from modification and replacement, while the rest of the stack can still change. A temporary override policy can be supplied with an update when a database change is really needed.",
      "Termination protection prevents deleting the stack. It does not stop updates.",
      "DeletionPolicy affects deletion, not updates. An update could still modify or replace the database.",
      "Denying all stack updates also blocks the frequent web tier changes."] },

{ id: "d3-11", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack creates an Auto Scaling group whose instances install an application through user data. The stack reports CREATE_COMPLETE before the application is installed, and sometimes the installation fails without the stack noticing.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer make the stack wait for a successful installation?",
  o: ["Add a CreationPolicy to the group, and run cfn-signal at the end of the user data script.",
      "Add a DependsOn attribute to the group so that it waits for the user data script to finish.",
      "Add a stack output that checks the application URL so that the stack waits for a response.",
      "Increase the stack's timeout so that CloudFormation waits longer before it reports success."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A CreationPolicy makes CloudFormation wait for a set number of success signals within a timeout before it marks the resource complete. cfn-signal sends success or failure based on the script's exit code, and a failure or timeout makes the stack roll back.",
      "DependsOn orders resource creation inside the stack. It cannot see whether a script inside an instance has finished.",
      "Outputs are values that the stack returns. They do not test URLs or make the stack wait.",
      "The stack timeout limits how long the stack can take. It does not make CloudFormation wait for the installation."] },

{ id: "d3-12", d: 3,
  q: "Instances launched by a CloudFormation stack are configured by cfn-init from AWS::CloudFormation::Init metadata. A CloudOps engineer changes a configuration file in the metadata and updates the stack, but the running instances do not get the new file.\n\nWhat should the engineer do so that running instances apply metadata changes automatically?",
  o: ["Run the cfn-hup daemon on the instances to detect metadata changes and rerun cfn-init.",
      "Add the configuration file to the user data so that it runs again on every stack update.",
      "Turn on drift detection so that CloudFormation pushes the new file to the instances.",
      "Add an UpdatePolicy to the instances so that cfn-init reads the metadata on each boot."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["cfn-hup is a helper daemon that polls the resource metadata for changes. When it detects a change, it runs the actions in its hooks, typically cfn-init, so running instances apply the new configuration without being replaced.",
      "User data runs only at first launch by default, so changing it does not update running instances.",
      "Drift detection reports differences. It does not change resources or push files.",
      "UpdatePolicy applies to resources such as Auto Scaling groups during updates. It does not make instances rerun cfn-init."] },

{ id: "d3-13", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack that contains a Lambda-backed custom resource has been in the CREATE_IN_PROGRESS state for more than 50 minutes. The Lambda function's logs show that it ran successfully and then exited.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The function did not send a response to the presigned S3 URL in the request.",
      "The function's execution role does not allow it to write logs to CloudWatch Logs.",
      "The stack's service role is missing permission to invoke the Lambda function.",
      "The custom resource type name does not start with the prefix AWS::Custom::."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFormation waits for the custom resource to send SUCCESS or FAILED to the presigned Amazon S3 response URL. If the function exits without sending a response, the stack waits until the timeout, which defaults to 1 hour. The ServiceTimeout property of the custom resource can shorten this wait.",
      "The logs show that the function ran and wrote logs, so its log permissions are fine.",
      "The function was invoked, as the logs show, so invocation permissions are not the problem.",
      "Custom resource types use the Custom:: prefix or AWS::CloudFormation::CustomResource. A wrong type name would fail validation at once, not hang."] },

{ id: "d3-14", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack update failed, and the rollback also failed because someone deleted one of the stack's resources outside CloudFormation. The stack is now in the UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED state.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to return the stack to a working state?",
  o: ["Continue the update rollback, and skip the resource that was deleted outside CloudFormation.",
      "Delete the stack, and create a new stack from the original template with the same name.",
      "Run drift detection on the stack, and then execute the most recent change set again.",
      "Update the stack again with the new template, because updates are allowed in this state."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["From UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED, you can continue the update rollback and list resources to skip, such as one that no longer exists. CloudFormation marks the skipped resources as complete and finishes the rollback to UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE, after which the stack can be updated again.",
      "Deleting and re-creating the stack is disruptive and could delete production resources that still work.",
      "Drift detection only reports differences, and a change set cannot be executed while the stack is in UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED.",
      "A stack in UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED cannot be updated until its rollback is continued and completes."] },

{ id: "d3-15", d: 3,
  q: "A networking stack exports its VPC ID, and three application stacks read it with Fn::ImportValue. A CloudOps engineer tries to update the networking stack to rename the export, and the update fails.\n\nWhy does the update fail?",
  o: ["An export cannot be changed or removed while another stack imports it.",
      "Exports can be renamed only by deleting and re-creating the networking stack.",
      "Fn::ImportValue works only between stacks that are nested in the same parent.",
      "The networking stack needs termination protection before exports can change."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFormation blocks changes to, and deletion of, an output export that other stacks import. The engineer must first update the importing stacks to stop using the export, and then change it.",
      "Re-creating the networking stack is not required, and deleting it would also be blocked while the export is in use.",
      "Fn::ImportValue works between any stacks in the same account and Region. Nested stacks are not required.",
      "Termination protection prevents stack deletion. It has nothing to do with changing exports."] },

{ id: "d3-16", d: 3,
  q: "Several teams copy the same 300 lines of CloudFormation for a standard load balancer and Auto Scaling setup into their own templates. When the standard changes, each copy must be updated by hand, and some copies drift from the standard.\n\nWhich solution will reduce this duplication?",
  o: ["Move the standard setup into its own template, and use it in each stack as a nested stack.",
      "Add the standard setup to a Mappings section that every template copies from a shared file.",
      "Use Fn::ImportValue to import the 300 lines of resources from a stack that one team owns.",
      "Turn on drift detection for every stack so that the copies stay in sync with the standard."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Nested stacks let a template include another template through an AWS::CloudFormation::Stack resource. The standard setup lives in one template that every team uses as a nested stack, so a change to the standard is made in one place.",
      "Mappings hold static lookup values. They cannot hold resources, and copying a file still duplicates it.",
      "Fn::ImportValue imports output values, such as IDs. It cannot import resource definitions.",
      "Drift detection compares stacks with their own templates. It does not keep templates in sync with each other."] },

{ id: "d3-17", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation template creates an Amazon RDS DB instance. The master password is passed as a plaintext parameter, and it shows up in deployment logs. The password is already stored in AWS Secrets Manager.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer supply the password without exposing it?",
  o: ["Use a dynamic reference such as {{resolve:secretsmanager:prod-db:SecretString:password}}.",
      "Store the password in the template's Mappings section, and mark the mapping as encrypted.",
      "Pass the password as a parameter with a default value so that it does not appear in the console.",
      "Put the password in an exported stack output, and import it into the DB instance resource."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Dynamic references retrieve values from Secrets Manager or Systems Manager Parameter Store while the stack operation runs. CloudFormation does not store or display the resolved secret value. Setting ManageMasterUserPassword so that RDS manages the secret in Secrets Manager is another option.",
      "Mappings are plain text in the template, and there is no encryption setting for them.",
      "Default values are stored in plain text in the template, which spreads the secret further.",
      "Outputs are visible to anyone who can describe the stack, so this exposes the password further."] },

{ id: "d3-18", d: 3,
  q: "Over several years, a company created dozens of resources by hand in the console. The company now wants to manage these existing resources with AWS CloudFormation without re-creating them.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Use the CloudFormation IaC generator to scan the resources, generate a template, and import them into a stack.",
      "Write a new template from scratch, deploy it as a stack, and delete the original resources after testing.",
      "Turn on drift detection in the account so that CloudFormation adopts resources that are not in a stack.",
      "Use AWS Config to export the resource configurations, and upload the export as a CloudFormation stack."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The IaC generator scans existing resources in the account and generates a template for the resources that you select. You can then import those resources into a new stack, so CloudFormation manages them without re-creating them.",
      "Re-creating resources causes downtime and data migration work, which the company wants to avoid.",
      "Drift detection works only on resources that are already in a stack. It does not adopt unmanaged resources.",
      "Config configuration items are not CloudFormation templates, and uploading them would not create a valid stack."] },

{ id: "d3-19", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer runs cdk deploy for the first time in a new AWS account and Region. The deployment fails with an error saying that the stack uses assets and that the toolkit stack must be deployed to the environment.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Run cdk bootstrap for that account and Region, and then run cdk deploy again.",
      "Run cdk synth to create a template, and deploy it by hand in the CloudFormation console.",
      "Run cdk diff to see the asset differences, and then add the assets to the stack by hand.",
      "Create an S3 bucket with the same name as the stack, and then run cdk deploy again."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Bootstrapping deploys the CDKToolkit stack, which creates the S3 bucket, ECR repository, and IAM roles that the CDK uses to upload assets and deploy stacks. Each account and Region must be bootstrapped once before you deploy CDK apps that use assets.",
      "The synthesized template still references assets that must be uploaded to the bootstrap resources, so a manual deployment would also fail.",
      "cdk diff only shows changes. It does not create the resources needed for assets.",
      "The CDK expects its own bootstrap resources and roles, not a bucket named after the stack."] },

{ id: "d3-20", d: 3,
  q: "A team manages infrastructure with the AWS CDK. Before each deployment, a reviewer must see exactly which AWS resources and IAM permissions will change compared with the deployed stack.\n\nWhich CDK command should the team run?",
  o: ["cdk diff",
      "cdk synth",
      "cdk doctor",
      "cdk bootstrap"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["cdk diff compares the synthesized template with the deployed stack and lists added, changed, and removed resources. It also highlights security-related changes such as IAM policy statements and security group rules.",
      "cdk synth generates the CloudFormation template, but it does not compare the template with the deployed stack.",
      "cdk doctor checks the local CDK setup for problems. It does not show infrastructure changes.",
      "cdk bootstrap prepares an environment for deployments. It does not preview changes."] },

{ id: "d3-21", d: 3,
  q: "A team keeps CloudFormation templates and parameter files in a GitHub repository. The team wants stacks to update automatically whenever changes are merged into the main branch, without building a CI/CD pipeline.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement?",
  o: ["Turn on CloudFormation Git sync for each stack, and link it to the repository and branch.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule for GitHub commits that runs cfn-init on the stack's instances.",
      "Set up S3 Event Notifications on a bucket that GitHub copies the templates into after merges.",
      "Turn on drift detection for each stack so that changes in GitHub are applied automatically."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Git sync connects a stack to a Git repository through an AWS CodeConnections connection. When a commit changes the template or deployment file on the tracked branch, CloudFormation updates the stack automatically.",
      "cfn-init configures software on instances. It does not update stacks, and GitHub commits do not arrive as EventBridge events by default.",
      "GitHub does not copy files to a bucket on its own, and this would still need custom code to update the stacks.",
      "Drift detection compares resources with their templates. It does not watch a Git repository."] },

{ id: "d3-22", d: 3,
  q: "An Auto Scaling group fails to launch instances during scale-out. The activity history shows that the subnets do not have enough free IP addresses. The group uses one /28 subnet in each of two Availability Zones.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Add larger subnets to the VPC, and add them to the Auto Scaling group.",
      "Change to a larger instance type that needs fewer IP addresses in total.",
      "Turn on auto-assign public IPv4 addresses for the existing subnets.",
      "Release the five IP addresses that AWS reserves in each subnet."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A /28 subnet has 16 addresses, and AWS reserves 5 addresses in every subnet, so only 11 are usable. Adding larger subnets, after adding a secondary CIDR block to the VPC if needed, gives the group room to launch instances.",
      "Every instance still needs at least one private IP address, so larger instances do not fix the shortage, and they cost more.",
      "Public IPv4 addresses do not replace the private addresses that each instance needs from the subnet.",
      "AWS uses the five reserved addresses for the network address, the VPC router, DNS, future use, and broadcast. They cannot be released."] },

{ id: "d3-23", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer's IAM user can create CloudFormation stacks. A stack that launches an EC2 instance with an instance profile fails with an error saying that the user is not authorized to perform iam:PassRole on the role.\n\nWhat should be done to resolve the error while following the principle of least privilege?",
  o: ["Allow iam:PassRole on that specific role's ARN in the engineer's IAM policy.",
      "Attach the AdministratorAccess managed policy to the engineer's IAM user.",
      "Add the engineer's IAM user as a principal in the trust policy of the instance profile role.",
      "Remove the instance profile from the template so that no role is needed."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Passing a role to a service, such as attaching an instance profile to an EC2 instance, requires iam:PassRole on that role. Allowing it only on the specific role ARN, optionally with an iam:PassedToService condition for ec2.amazonaws.com, follows least privilege.",
      "Administrator access fixes the error but grants far more than is needed.",
      "The trust policy decides who can assume the role. Amazon EC2 assumes it here, and the user needs permission to pass it, not to assume it.",
      "Removing the instance profile breaks the application's access to AWS services."] },

{ id: "d3-24", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack that creates a new VPC fails with the error The maximum number of VPCs has been reached. The account already has 5 VPCs in the Region, and all of them are in use.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Request a VPC quota increase for the Region through Service Quotas, and retry the stack.",
      "Delete the default VPC's subnets so that the VPC no longer counts toward the quota.",
      "Add a DependsOn attribute so that the VPC is created after the other resources in the stack.",
      "Change the VPC's CIDR block to a smaller range so that it fits within the current quota."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The default quota is 5 VPCs per Region, and it is adjustable. Requesting an increase through Service Quotas lets the stack create the VPC once the increase is approved.",
      "Deleting subnets does not remove the VPC, so it still counts toward the quota.",
      "The order in which resources are created does not change the account's VPC quota.",
      "The quota counts VPCs, not address space, so a smaller CIDR block does not help."] },

{ id: "d3-25", d: 3,
  q: "During a large scaling event, an Auto Scaling group that uses a single instance type in one Availability Zone fails to launch instances. The activity history shows InsufficientInstanceCapacity errors.\n\nWhich change will MOST improve the group's ability to launch instances?",
  o: ["Configure the group to use several instance types across multiple Availability Zones.",
      "Increase the group's maximum capacity so that Auto Scaling retries more launches at once.",
      "Request a higher quota for On-Demand Instances of that type through Service Quotas.",
      "Change the group's health check type from EC2 to ELB so that failed launches are replaced."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["InsufficientInstanceCapacity means that AWS does not have enough capacity of that instance type in that Availability Zone at that moment. Allowing more instance types and more zones gives Auto Scaling other capacity pools to launch from. On-Demand Capacity Reservations can guarantee capacity for planned events.",
      "Raising the maximum does not create capacity, so more launches would fail in the same way.",
      "This error is about AWS capacity in the zone, not the account's quota. A quota error would say that a limit was exceeded.",
      "The health check type affects how running instances are judged. It does not help instances launch."] },

{ id: "d3-26", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation template creates an S3 bucket with the BucketName property set to app-logs. The stack fails in the first account where the team deploys it, with an error that the bucket already exists.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do so that the template can be deployed in any account and Region?",
  o: ["Remove the BucketName property so that CloudFormation generates a unique bucket name.",
      "Delete any existing bucket called app-logs in the account, and deploy the stack again.",
      "Add a DeletionPolicy of Retain to the bucket so that the name is reserved for the stack.",
      "Create the stack with termination protection so that the bucket name is always available."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 bucket names in the general purpose namespace are globally unique across all AWS accounts, so a fixed name such as app-logs is almost certainly taken. If BucketName is left out, CloudFormation generates a unique name that other resources can reference with Ref. A name can also be built from the account ID and Region.",
      "The existing bucket is probably in another AWS account, so it cannot be deleted from this account.",
      "A Retain policy affects what happens on deletion. It does not reserve a name.",
      "Termination protection prevents the stack from being deleted. It has no effect on bucket names."] },

{ id: "d3-27", d: 3,
  q: "A launch template uses EBS volumes that are encrypted with a customer managed KMS key. When the Auto Scaling group launches instances, they move to the terminated state almost immediately with the reason Client.InternalError: Client error on launch.\n\nWhat is the MOST likely cause?",
  o: ["The Auto Scaling service-linked role does not have permission to use the KMS key.",
      "The launch template does not specify an IAM instance profile for the new instances.",
      "The subnet for the Auto Scaling group does not have a route to an internet gateway.",
      "The KMS key has automatic key rotation turned on, which blocks new EBS volumes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Auto Scaling launches instances by using its service-linked role, AWSServiceRoleForAutoScaling. If the key policy does not allow that role to use the customer managed key, EBS cannot create the encrypted volumes, and the instances terminate at once with this error. Adding the role to the key policy, or creating a grant, fixes it.",
      "Instances can launch without an instance profile. A missing profile affects the application's AWS access, not the launch.",
      "Private subnets without an internet route can still launch instances. Routing does not cause this launch error.",
      "Key rotation does not block use of the key. Older key material stays available for decryption."] },

{ id: "d3-28", d: 3,
  q: "A company uses AWS Organizations. The security team must deploy a standard set of IAM roles and an AWS Config rule to every account in the Workloads OU, including accounts that are added later.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a StackSet with service-managed permissions that targets the OU and has automatic deployment turned on.",
      "Create a StackSet with self-managed permissions, and add each new account's ID to the StackSet when it joins.",
      "Write a Lambda function that runs when an account is created and deploys the stack by assuming a role in it.",
      "Ask each account owner to deploy the template from a shared S3 bucket when their account is created."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With service-managed permissions, StackSets uses Organizations to create the required roles for you. Targeting the OU with automatic deployment turned on deploys stack instances to accounts that join the OU later, and it can remove them from accounts that leave.",
      "Self-managed permissions require you to create administration and execution roles in every account, and each new account must be added by hand.",
      "Custom code can work, but StackSets provides this behavior natively.",
      "Relying on account owners is slow and error prone, and some accounts would be missed."] },

{ id: "d3-29", d: 3,
  q: "A company does not use AWS Organizations. A CloudOps engineer wants to deploy a template to 10 accounts by using a StackSet with self-managed permissions. The stack instances fail with an error that the StackSet could not assume a role in the target accounts.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Create the AWSCloudFormationStackSetExecutionRole in each target account, and trust the administrator account.",
      "Turn on trusted access for StackSets in AWS Organizations so that the roles are created automatically.",
      "Share the template with each target account through AWS RAM, and then retry the stack instances.",
      "Add the target account IDs to the template's Parameters section, and run the operation again."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Self-managed StackSets need an administration role in the administrator account (AWSCloudFormationStackSetAdministrationRole) and an execution role in each target account (AWSCloudFormationStackSetExecutionRole) that trusts it. Without the execution role, StackSets cannot assume a role to create the stack.",
      "Trusted access and service-managed permissions require AWS Organizations, which the company does not use.",
      "StackSets does not need the template to be shared. The failure is about assuming a role.",
      "Target accounts are chosen when you create stack instances, not in template parameters, and this does not create the missing roles."] },

{ id: "d3-30", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer must roll out an update through a StackSet to 200 accounts in 4 Regions. The rollout must update no more than 10 accounts at a time and must stop if more than 2 accounts fail in a Region.\n\nWhich StackSet settings should the engineer use?",
  o: ["Set maximum concurrent accounts to 10 and failure tolerance to 2.",
      "Set maximum concurrent accounts to 100% and failure tolerance to 0.",
      "Set Region concurrency to parallel and failure tolerance to 100%.",
      "Create 20 StackSets with 10 accounts each, and run them in turn."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Operation preferences control rollout speed and safety. Maximum concurrent accounts limits how many accounts are updated at the same time in each Region, and failure tolerance sets how many failures are allowed in a Region before the operation stops. In the default strict mode, the actual concurrency starts at the lower of 10 and the failure tolerance plus 1, which stays within the limit.",
      "100% concurrency updates every account at once, and a tolerance of 0 stops after the first failure, so neither requirement is met.",
      "A tolerance of 100% never stops the rollout, and parallel Regions do not limit how many accounts are updated at once.",
      "Twenty StackSets add a lot of management effort, and they would still need operation preferences to stop on failures."] },

{ id: "d3-31", d: 3,
  q: "A network team owns a central VPC in a networking account. Application teams in other accounts in the same organization must launch EC2 instances and RDS databases into subnets of that VPC. The network team must keep control of routing and network ACLs.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Share the subnets with the application accounts by using AWS Resource Access Manager.",
      "Create VPC peering connections between the central VPC and a VPC in each application account.",
      "Create a cross-account IAM role in the networking account for the application teams to assume.",
      "Use a CloudFormation StackSet to copy the central VPC into each of the application accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["VPC sharing uses AWS RAM to share subnets with other accounts. Participants launch their own resources into the shared subnets, while the owner account keeps control of the VPC, route tables, network ACLs, and gateways.",
      "Peering connects separate VPCs. The application resources would live in their own VPCs, not in the central VPC's subnets.",
      "Assuming a role in the networking account would create the application resources in that account, which mixes ownership and billing.",
      "Copying the VPC creates separate VPCs in each account, so the network team would not control a single shared network."] },

{ id: "d3-32", d: 3,
  q: "A shared services account has Route 53 Resolver forwarding rules that send queries for corp.example.com to on-premises DNS servers. Fifty VPCs in other accounts in the organization must use the same rules.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Share the Resolver rules through AWS RAM, and associate them with the VPCs in each account.",
      "Create an outbound endpoint and identical forwarding rules in every account and every VPC.",
      "Create a private hosted zone named corp.example.com, and associate it with all 50 VPCs.",
      "Change the DHCP options set in each VPC to use the on-premises DNS servers directly."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resolver rules can be shared with other accounts through AWS RAM. The other accounts associate the shared rules with their VPCs, and queries are forwarded through the outbound endpoint in the shared services account, so there is only one set of rules and endpoints to maintain.",
      "Duplicating endpoints and rules in every account adds cost and makes changes harder.",
      "A private hosted zone would answer the queries itself in AWS. It would not forward them to the on-premises DNS servers.",
      "Pointing VPCs directly at on-premises DNS servers breaks resolution of AWS private names and makes every query depend on the hybrid link."] },

{ id: "d3-33", d: 3,
  q: "Developers must be able to launch a standard, approved environment from a CloudFormation template on their own. The developers must not have IAM permissions to create the underlying resources, such as VPCs and IAM roles, directly.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Publish the template as a Service Catalog product with a launch constraint role that has the needed permissions.",
      "Give the developers the AdministratorAccess policy, and ask them to deploy only the approved template.",
      "Share the template in an S3 bucket, and let the developers deploy it with their own IAM permissions.",
      "Create a StackSet in the management account that deploys the environment into every developer account daily."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Service Catalog lets administrators publish approved products in portfolios. A launch constraint names an IAM role that Service Catalog uses to provision the product, so developers need permission only to launch the product, not to create each resource.",
      "Administrator access breaks the requirement that developers cannot create the resources directly.",
      "Deploying with their own permissions requires developers to have rights to create VPCs and IAM roles.",
      "Daily deployments into every account do not let developers launch environments on demand, and they create environments that might not be needed."] },

{ id: "d3-34", d: 3,
  q: "A company runs an Amazon ECS service behind an Application Load Balancer. Each new version must run alongside the current version and be tested before it receives production traffic. The company must be able to roll back quickly.\n\nWhich deployment approach will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use an ECS blue/green deployment that tests the new version before it shifts production traffic.",
      "Use an ECS rolling update with a minimum healthy percent of 100 and a maximum percent of 200.",
      "Stop the service, update the task definition, and start the service again during a maintenance window.",
      "Increase the service's desired count, and register the new task definition with the same target group."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["ECS blue/green deployments start the new revision (green) alongside the current one (blue) and can send test traffic to it first. Lifecycle hooks can run validation, production traffic then shifts, and ECS can roll back to blue quickly if CloudWatch alarms fire. ECS supports this natively, and CodeDeploy can also orchestrate it.",
      "A rolling update replaces tasks gradually behind the same listener, so there is no separate test traffic path, and rolling back means another rolling deployment.",
      "Stopping the service causes downtime and gives no way to test before release.",
      "Mixing new tasks into the same target group sends production traffic to them right away, with no validation step or quick rollback."] },

{ id: "d3-35", d: 3,
  q: "A team deploys a new version of a Lambda function that handles payments. The team wants 10% of traffic to go to the new version for 10 minutes and the rest to shift automatically if no errors occur. The release must roll back automatically if a CloudWatch alarm fires.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use CodeDeploy with the LambdaCanary10Percent10Minutes configuration and a rollback alarm on the alias.",
      "Publish the new version, and update the alias to send all traffic to it after testing it in the console.",
      "Create a second function for the new code, and use Route 53 weighted records to split the traffic.",
      "Increase the provisioned concurrency of the new version so that it can handle 10% of the traffic."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CodeDeploy shifts traffic between versions on a Lambda alias. The canary configuration sends 10% of traffic to the new version for 10 minutes and then shifts the rest, and a CloudWatch alarm on the deployment triggers an automatic rollback.",
      "Moving all traffic at once is not a canary release, and there is no automatic rollback.",
      "Route 53 weighted records apply to DNS names, not to direct Lambda invocations, and this setup has no automatic rollback.",
      "Provisioned concurrency reduces cold starts. It does not split or shift traffic between versions."] },

{ id: "d3-36", d: 3,
  q: "A company releases a new AMI for a 40-instance Auto Scaling group. The company wants to replace instances gradually, keep at least 90% of capacity healthy, and pause after the first 25% of instances so that the team can verify the release.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Start an instance refresh with a minimum healthy percentage of 90 and a checkpoint at 25%.",
      "Update the launch template, and terminate 10 instances by hand every hour until all are new.",
      "Create a new Auto Scaling group with the new AMI, and delete the old group after it launches.",
      "Update the launch template, and wait for the group to replace instances during normal scaling."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An instance refresh replaces instances in rolling batches and keeps at least the minimum healthy percentage in service. A checkpoint pauses the refresh after a set percentage of instances is replaced, so the team can verify before it continues. Auto rollback can also be turned on.",
      "Manual terminations are slow and error prone, and there is no built-in pause or health threshold.",
      "Deleting the old group after launching a new one does not give a gradual, verified rollout, and it needs traffic switching work.",
      "Normal scaling might never replace some instances, so the fleet would run mixed versions for a long time."] },

{ id: "d3-37", d: 3,
  q: "A faulty container image was deployed to an Amazon ECS service. The new tasks kept failing their health checks, and the deployment kept starting new tasks for hours until an engineer noticed.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure so that ECS detects failed deployments and returns to the last working version automatically?",
  o: ["Turn on the ECS deployment circuit breaker with the rollback option.",
      "Set the service's minimum healthy percent to 0 during deployments.",
      "Turn on ECS Exec so that engineers can connect to the failing tasks.",
      "Increase the health check grace period of the service to 2 hours."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The deployment circuit breaker detects when a rolling deployment cannot reach a steady state, for example when new tasks keep failing health checks. With rollback turned on, ECS returns the service to the last completed deployment automatically.",
      "A minimum healthy percent of 0 lets ECS stop all old tasks during a deployment, which can cause an outage.",
      "ECS Exec helps with debugging inside containers. It does not detect failed deployments or roll back.",
      "A longer grace period makes ECS wait longer before it acts on failed health checks, which makes the problem worse."] },

{ id: "d3-38", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudFormation stack update changes a web application's configuration. The company wants CloudFormation to roll back the update automatically if the application's 5XX error alarm goes into ALARM during the update or within 15 minutes after it.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer configure?",
  o: ["Add the alarm as a rollback trigger on the stack with a monitoring time of 15 minutes.",
      "Add a stack policy that denies updates whenever the 5XX error alarm is in the ALARM state.",
      "Add a CreationPolicy that waits 15 minutes for a signal from the 5XX error alarm on update.",
      "Turn on termination protection so that a failing update cannot remove the healthy resources."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Rollback triggers make CloudFormation monitor CloudWatch alarms during a stack create or update and for a monitoring period afterward of up to 180 minutes. If an alarm goes into ALARM, CloudFormation rolls back the whole operation.",
      "Stack policies are static rules about which resources can be updated. They do not react to alarm states.",
      "A CreationPolicy waits for signals from instances during creation. CloudWatch alarms do not send these signals.",
      "Termination protection blocks stack deletion. It does not roll back failed updates."] },

{ id: "d3-39", d: 3,
  q: "Several engineers run Terraform against the same AWS environment from their laptops. The state file is stored locally, and twice an engineer has overwritten another engineer's changes.\n\nWhich solution will prevent these conflicts?",
  o: ["Store the state in an S3 backend with versioning, encryption, and state locking turned on.",
      "Commit the state file to the Git repository so that every engineer pulls the latest copy.",
      "Copy the state file to a shared network drive, and ask engineers to take turns running apply.",
      "Run terraform refresh before each apply so that each laptop has the newest local state."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A remote S3 backend keeps one shared state file, versioning lets you recover earlier states, and encryption protects secrets in the state. State locking, through the S3 backend's native lock file or the older DynamoDB table method, stops two runs from changing the state at the same time.",
      "State files can contain secrets, and Git does not stop two people from applying at the same time with different copies.",
      "Taking turns is a manual process with no lock, so conflicts can still happen.",
      "Refreshing updates each local copy, but it does not coordinate engineers or prevent simultaneous applies."] },

{ id: "d3-40", d: 3,
  q: "A team manages most of its infrastructure with Terraform. An S3 bucket was created by hand in the console and must now be managed by Terraform without being deleted or re-created.\n\nWhat should the team do?",
  o: ["Write a resource block for the bucket, and bring the bucket into state with terraform import or an import block.",
      "Run terraform apply with a new resource block so that Terraform adopts the existing bucket by its name.",
      "Delete the bucket, and let Terraform create a new bucket with the same name on the next apply.",
      "Run terraform state rm on the bucket so that Terraform stops tracking resources it did not create."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Import adds an existing resource to the Terraform state so that Terraform manages it from then on. After the import, terraform plan should show no changes if the resource block matches the real bucket.",
      "Without an import, apply tries to create a new bucket with the same name and fails because the bucket already exists.",
      "Deleting the bucket destroys its data, and the name might not be available again right away.",
      "state rm removes resources from the state. The bucket is not in the state, so this does nothing to help."] },

{ id: "d3-41", d: 3,
  q: "A GitHub Actions workflow runs Terraform to deploy to AWS. The workflow uses IAM user access keys that are stored as repository secrets. The security team wants to eliminate long-lived credentials.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Add GitHub as an OIDC identity provider in IAM, and let the workflow assume a role with temporary credentials.",
      "Rotate the IAM user's access keys every 30 days, and update the repository secrets after each rotation.",
      "Store the access keys in AWS Secrets Manager, and have the workflow read them at the start of each run.",
      "Create a separate IAM user for each workflow, and give each user only the permissions that it needs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["With an OIDC identity provider for GitHub, the workflow gets a short-lived token and exchanges it for temporary credentials by assuming an IAM role. The role's trust policy can limit access to a specific repository and branch, and no long-lived keys are stored.",
      "Rotation shortens how long each key is valid, but the keys are still long-lived credentials stored in GitHub.",
      "The workflow would still need credentials to read from Secrets Manager, and the access keys would still be long-lived.",
      "Separate users improve least privilege, but each one still has long-lived access keys."] },

{ id: "d3-42", d: 3,
  q: "A company has thousands of EC2 instances in 30 accounts and 4 Regions in AWS Organizations. The company wants to scan instances for missing patches daily and install approved security patches every Sunday, managed from one central configuration.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a patch policy in Quick Setup for Patch Manager that targets the organization and its Regions.",
      "Create a maintenance window and a patch baseline in each account and Region, and register the instances.",
      "Add a cron job to each instance's user data that runs the operating system package update command.",
      "Build a new AMI each Sunday with the latest patches, and replace every instance in the organization."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Patch policies, created through Quick Setup, configure Patch Manager scan and install schedules and patch baselines across an organization's accounts and Regions from one place.",
      "This works, but it must be built and maintained in 120 account and Region combinations.",
      "Cron jobs on each instance are hard to manage, give no compliance reporting, and do not use approved baselines.",
      "Replacing thousands of instances every week is disruptive and not feasible for every workload."] },

{ id: "d3-43", d: 3,
  q: "A company wants Patch Manager to install security updates on production Linux instances only after the updates have been available for 7 days, so that problems can be found in testing first. Patches must then be approved automatically.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer configure Patch Manager?",
  o: ["Create a custom patch baseline with a rule that auto-approves security patches 7 days after release.",
      "Use the default predefined patch baseline, and schedule the patch installation to run every 7 days.",
      "Create a patch group that excludes patches from the last 7 days by listing each one as rejected.",
      "Turn on Amazon Inspector, and patch only CVEs that Inspector has reported for at least 7 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Patch baseline approval rules can auto-approve patches by classification and severity a set number of days after they are released. A 7-day delay for security patches gives time for testing before production installs them.",
      "Running the installation every 7 days does not delay approval. A patch released the day before could still be installed.",
      "Rejecting each new patch by hand is constant manual work and easy to get wrong.",
      "Inspector finds vulnerabilities but does not control patch approval timing, and this would still need a patching process."] },

{ id: "d3-44", d: 3,
  q: "Company policy requires that a specific security agent is installed and running on every EC2 instance. Engineers sometimes stop the agent while troubleshooting and forget to start it again.\n\nWhich solution will keep the agent in the required state automatically?",
  o: ["Create a State Manager association that installs and starts the agent on a schedule.",
      "Run a Run Command document once to install the agent on all instances in the fleet.",
      "Add the agent installation to the AMI so that it is present on every new instance.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that reports instances where the agent is not running."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A State Manager association applies a document to targets on a schedule, such as every 30 minutes, and reports compliance. If someone stops the agent, the next run installs or starts it again.",
      "A one-time command installs the agent once but does not fix it when someone stops it later.",
      "Baking the agent into the AMI covers new instances, but it does not restart the agent on running instances.",
      "Config can report the problem, but reporting alone does not keep the agent running."] },

{ id: "d3-45", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer launches EC2 instances in a private subnet that has no NAT gateway or other internet access. The instances use an AMI with SSM Agent preinstalled, but they do not appear as managed nodes in Systems Manager Fleet Manager.\n\nWhich combination of actions will resolve the issue? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Attach an instance profile that includes the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy.",
      "Create interface VPC endpoints for the Systems Manager services, such as ssm and ssmmessages.",
      "Open inbound port 22 on the instance security group from the Systems Manager service.",
      "Assign an Elastic IP address to each instance so that Systems Manager can reach it.",
      "Install the CloudWatch agent so that the instances report their status to Fleet Manager."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["SSM Agent needs AWS credentials with Systems Manager permissions, which the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore managed policy provides through an instance profile. Default Host Management Configuration is another way to provide them.",
      "SSM Agent connects outbound to Systems Manager endpoints over HTTPS. Without internet access, interface endpoints for ssm and ssmmessages (and ec2messages for older agent versions) give the agent a private path. The endpoint security group must allow HTTPS from the instances.",
      "Systems Manager does not connect inbound to instances, and it does not use SSH. The agent makes outbound connections.",
      "Elastic IP addresses do not help in a subnet with no route to an internet gateway, and Systems Manager does not need inbound access.",
      "The CloudWatch agent publishes metrics and logs. Managed node registration depends on SSM Agent, credentials, and network access."] },

{ id: "d3-46", d: 3,
  q: "A company has hundreds of EC2 instances in one account, and many were launched without instance profiles. The company wants every instance in the Region to be managed by Systems Manager without attaching an instance profile to each instance.\n\nWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Turn on Default Host Management Configuration for the account and Region.",
      "Create a hybrid activation, and register each EC2 instance as a hybrid node.",
      "Attach the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy to each user who launches instances.",
      "Add the ssm:* permission to the default VPC security group that the instances use."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Default Host Management Configuration lets Systems Manager manage EC2 instances automatically through an account-level IAM role, without an instance profile on each instance. The instances need IMDSv2 and a supported version of SSM Agent.",
      "Hybrid activations are for servers outside AWS. Registering EC2 instances this way adds work and cost that are not needed.",
      "Permissions on users do not give SSM Agent on the instances any credentials.",
      "Security groups control network traffic, not IAM permissions."] },

{ id: "d3-47", d: 3,
  q: "A company wants to patch and run commands on 300 on-premises Linux servers by using the same AWS Systems Manager tools that it uses for its EC2 instances.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do?",
  o: ["Create a hybrid activation, and install SSM Agent on each server with the activation code and ID.",
      "Attach an EC2 instance profile to each on-premises server so that SSM Agent can use its role credentials.",
      "Install the CloudWatch agent on each server, and send Run Command documents through CloudWatch.",
      "Open inbound port 443 on each server so that Systems Manager can push commands to the servers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A hybrid activation creates an activation code and ID that are tied to an IAM service role. Installing SSM Agent with them registers each server as a managed node, so Run Command, Patch Manager, and the other tools work the same way as they do on EC2.",
      "Instance profiles exist only for EC2 instances. On-premises servers get credentials through a hybrid activation.",
      "The CloudWatch agent collects metrics and logs. It does not run Systems Manager commands.",
      "SSM Agent makes outbound HTTPS connections to Systems Manager. Systems Manager does not push commands through inbound ports."] },

{ id: "d3-48", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer needs to run a shell script once on 500 Linux instances that are tagged Role=Web to change a local log configuration. The output of every run must be kept for later review, and SSH access is not allowed.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST effort?",
  o: ["Use Run Command with AWS-RunShellScript, target the Role=Web tag, and send output to an S3 bucket.",
      "Create an EC2 Image Builder pipeline that adds the script to a new AMI, and replace all of the instances.",
      "Add the script to each instance's user data, and reboot the instances so that the script runs again.",
      "Use EC2 Instance Connect to open a session to each instance, and save the output in a document."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Run Command runs a document on many managed nodes at once, selected by tag, with rate controls and no SSH. Command output can be written to Amazon S3 or CloudWatch Logs for review.",
      "Building a new AMI and replacing instances is far more work than running one script once.",
      "User data runs only on first boot by default, so a reboot does not run it again, and the output is not kept centrally.",
      "Instance Connect uses SSH, which is not allowed, and connecting to 500 instances by hand is slow."] },

{ id: "d3-49", d: 3,
  q: "The security team asks for a report of which software packages and versions are installed on every EC2 instance across 20 accounts. The report must be queryable with SQL and must stay up to date automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on Systems Manager Inventory, and use a resource data sync to an S3 bucket that Athena can query.",
      "Run a script on each instance every day that emails its package list to the security team.",
      "Turn on AWS Config recording for EC2 instances, and download each configuration item as a CSV file.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events for Amazon EC2, and search the trail for package install API calls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Inventory collects metadata such as installed applications and versions from managed nodes. A resource data sync gathers inventory from many accounts and Regions into one S3 bucket, where Amazon Athena can query it with SQL.",
      "Daily emails from each instance are hard to combine and query, and the script must be maintained.",
      "Downloading configuration items by hand does not give an automatic, SQL-queryable report across accounts, and software data comes from Systems Manager anyway.",
      "Installing a package inside an operating system is not an AWS API call, so CloudTrail does not record it."] },

{ id: "d3-50", d: 3,
  q: "An application has 80 configuration values for each of three environments: dev, test, and prod. Developers store them as one long flat list of parameter names, and loading the right set for an environment is error prone. Some of the values are secrets.\n\nHow should a CloudOps engineer organize the parameters?",
  o: ["Use a path hierarchy such as /app/prod/db-url, and use SecureString for the secret values.",
      "Store all values for each environment in one String parameter as a large JSON document.",
      "Store each value as a separate secret in AWS Secrets Manager with the environment in a tag.",
      "Put all values in environment variables in the AMI so that every instance has the full set."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Hierarchical names group parameters by application and environment, and GetParametersByPath loads every parameter under /app/prod/ in one call. IAM policies can grant access by path, and SecureString encrypts the secret values with AWS KMS.",
      "One large document is hard to update safely and can exceed the 4 KB size limit of standard parameters. The secrets would also be stored as plain text.",
      "Secrets Manager suits secrets that need rotation, but storing 240 ordinary configuration values there costs more and is not needed.",
      "Values in an AMI are hard to change, are visible to anyone on the instance, and need a new AMI for each change."] },

{ id: "d3-51", d: 3,
  q: "Users upload images and PDF files to the uploads/ prefix of an S3 bucket. A Lambda function must create a thumbnail for every .jpg file within seconds of upload. Other files must not invoke the function.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Add an S3 event notification for object creation with prefix uploads/ and suffix .jpg that invokes the function.",
      "Schedule the function every minute to list the bucket and create thumbnails for any new .jpg files.",
      "Turn on S3 Inventory each day, and invoke the function for each .jpg file listed in the report.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail data events, and invoke the function from a metric filter on PutObject calls."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["S3 Event Notifications can invoke a Lambda function directly when objects are created, with prefix and suffix filters so that only .jpg files under uploads/ trigger it. Events usually arrive within seconds.",
      "Polling the bucket every minute is slower, costs more as the bucket grows, and must track which files were already processed.",
      "S3 Inventory reports are produced daily or weekly, which is far too slow.",
      "Metric filters create metrics and cannot pass object details to a function, so this is slow and indirect."] },

{ id: "d3-52", d: 3,
  q: "When objects are created in an S3 bucket, three teams must each process them: one with a Lambda function, one with a Step Functions workflow, and one through an SQS queue. The third team wants only objects larger than 1 GB.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on Amazon EventBridge notifications for the bucket.",
      "Create EventBridge rules for each team, with a size filter in the pattern for the team that needs it.",
      "Create three S3 Event Notifications with the same event type and prefix, one for each target.",
      "Write a Lambda function that receives each S3 event and forwards it to the other two targets.",
      "Turn on S3 Replication to three buckets, one for each team, and configure events on each copy."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["When EventBridge notifications are turned on, Amazon S3 sends the bucket's events to the default event bus, where many rules can use them.",
      "EventBridge rules can match fields in the event, such as the object size with a numeric filter, and each rule can send to targets such as Lambda, Step Functions, and SQS. Each team gets its own routing without code.",
      "S3 Event Notifications cannot have overlapping configurations for the same event type and prefix, and they cannot filter by object size.",
      "A forwarding function is custom code to maintain and a single point of failure.",
      "Replicating every object three times adds storage cost and delay just to send events to three teams."] },

{ id: "d3-53", d: 3,
  q: "A Lambda function is invoked by S3 object creation events on a bucket. The function resizes each image and writes the result back to the same bucket. After deployment, invocations and costs grow out of control.\n\nWhat should a CloudOps engineer do to fix the root cause?",
  o: ["Write the resized images to a different bucket, or use a prefix filter that excludes the output prefix.",
      "Increase the function's reserved concurrency so that it can keep up with the number of events.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning so that S3 sends only one event notification for each object key that is written.",
      "Add a dead-letter queue to the function so that the extra invocations are moved into the queue."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Each resized image written to the same bucket creates a new event, which invokes the function again in a loop. Writing output to a separate bucket, or triggering only on an input prefix that the output never uses, breaks the loop. Lambda recursive loop detection can stop some loops, but the design still needs fixing.",
      "More concurrency lets the loop grow even faster and raises costs.",
      "Versioning does not reduce events. Every write still creates a new event.",
      "A dead-letter queue holds failed asynchronous invocations. These invocations succeed, so the loop continues."] },

{ id: "d3-54", d: 3,
  q: "A Lambda function processes messages from an SQS standard queue. The function's timeout is 5 minutes, and the queue's visibility timeout is 30 seconds. Some messages are processed several times, and a few malformed messages fail over and over.\n\nWhich combination of changes will resolve these issues? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Set the queue's visibility timeout to at least six times the function's timeout.",
      "Add a dead-letter queue with a redrive policy that sets a maxReceiveCount.",
      "Change the function's timeout to 30 seconds to match the visibility timeout.",
      "Turn on long polling for the queue by setting a receive wait time of 20 seconds.",
      "Increase the queue's message retention period from 4 days to 14 days."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["If the visibility timeout is shorter than the processing time, messages become visible again while the function is still working on them, so they are processed again. AWS recommends a visibility timeout of at least six times the function timeout for Lambda event source mappings.",
      "A redrive policy moves a message to a dead-letter queue after it has been received maxReceiveCount times without being deleted. Malformed messages stop retrying and can be examined separately.",
      "A shorter function timeout could cut off valid processing that needs more than 30 seconds.",
      "Long polling reduces empty receives and cost. It does not stop duplicates or failing messages.",
      "Longer retention keeps failing messages around even longer."] },

{ id: "d3-55", d: 3,
  q: "A monthly database maintenance process has five steps: take a snapshot, run a Lambda function to verify it, wait for a DBA to approve, apply a parameter change, and send a report. Each step must retry on failure, and the process must wait up to 24 hours for the approval.\n\nWhich solution will orchestrate this process with the LEAST custom code?",
  o: ["Build an AWS Step Functions state machine with retries and a callback task that waits for an approval token.",
      "Chain five Lambda functions that call each other directly, and sleep inside the approval function.",
      "Run a cron job on an EC2 instance that calls each step in order and polls a file for the approval.",
      "Create five EventBridge rules that each start the next step when the previous one writes a log."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Step Functions coordinates multi-step workflows with built-in retries and error handling. A task that uses the waitForTaskToken pattern pauses until an approver returns the token, and standard workflows can run for up to a year, so a 24-hour wait is fine.",
      "Lambda functions time out after 15 minutes, so a function cannot sleep for 24 hours, and chained calls make retries hard to manage.",
      "A server-based script must be patched and monitored, and it needs its own retry logic.",
      "Chaining rules through log messages is fragile and hard to follow, and it gives no clear way to wait for approval."] },

{ id: "d3-56", d: 3,
  q: "A company uses AWS DevOps Agent to investigate incidents. When an investigation completes, the company wants to create a ticket in its internal ticketing system through an HTTP API and post a summary to a chat channel automatically.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create an EventBridge rule for DevOps Agent investigation events that targets an API destination and the chat function.",
      "Have an engineer copy each finished investigation from the DevOps Agent web app into the ticketing system.",
      "Poll the DevOps Agent API every 5 minutes from an EC2 instance, and create tickets for any new results.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm on a DevOps Agent metric that opens a ticket whenever an investigation ends."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS DevOps Agent publishes investigation events to Amazon EventBridge for custom automation. A rule that matches completed investigations can call the ticketing system through an API destination and invoke the function that posts to chat, with no polling.",
      "Manual copying is slow and depends on someone remembering to do it.",
      "Polling from a server is custom code and infrastructure to maintain, and EventBridge already delivers the events.",
      "An alarm watches metric values. It cannot carry investigation details to create a ticket."] },

{ id: "d3-57", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer edits CloudFormation templates in Kiro. The engineer wants Kiro to run cfn-lint and fix simple problems automatically every time a template file is saved, without being asked each time.\n\nWhich Kiro feature should the engineer configure?",
  o: ["An agent hook that is triggered when a template file is saved",
      "A steering file that lists all of the cfn-lint rules for the project",
      "A spec that describes how the templates should be linted",
      "An MCP server that stores the most recent lint results"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Agent hooks run an agent action automatically when an event happens in the IDE, such as saving a file that matches a pattern. A hook on template saves can run cfn-lint and fix simple findings without a manual prompt.",
      "Steering files give Kiro persistent guidance, but they do not run anything when a file is saved.",
      "Specs plan the requirements, design, and tasks for a feature. They do not react to file events.",
      "MCP servers connect Kiro to external tools and data. Storing results does not run the linter on each save."] },

{ id: "d3-58", d: 3,
  q: "The finance team needs every new EC2 instance to be tagged with the IAM identity of the person who launched it, within minutes of launch, no matter how it was launched. CloudTrail is turned on in the account.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet this requirement? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an EventBridge rule that matches the RunInstances event from AWS API Call via CloudTrail.",
      "Target a Lambda function that reads userIdentity from the event and tags the new instances.",
      "Require every user to add an Owner tag in the console launch wizard before launching an instance.",
      "Run an AWS Config rule once a day that tags instances with the name of the account's root user.",
      "Add a user data script to the launch template that tags each instance with its instance profile."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["EventBridge receives CloudTrail management events as AWS API Call via CloudTrail events. A rule on ec2:RunInstances matches every launch, whether it comes from the console, the CLI, an SDK, or infrastructure as code.",
      "The event's userIdentity field shows who made the call, and responseElements lists the new instance IDs. A Lambda function can call CreateTags with that identity.",
      "Relying on users is not reliable, and it does not cover launches from the CLI, SDKs, or automation.",
      "The root user is not the person who launched the instance, and a daily rule does not tag within minutes.",
      "The instance profile identifies the instance's role, not the person who launched it, and not every launch uses this template."] },

{ id: "d3-59", d: 3,
  q: "Version 1 of an application runs in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. Version 2 is deployed to a second Auto Scaling group with its own target group. The company wants to shift 10%, then 50%, then 100% of traffic to version 2, and to switch back instantly if needed, without DNS changes.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use weighted target groups in the ALB listener rule, and change the weights at each step.",
      "Create a Route 53 weighted record for each group, and change the record weights at each step.",
      "Register the version 2 instances in the version 1 target group, and remove old instances gradually.",
      "Run an instance refresh on the version 1 group with checkpoints at 10% and 50% of the instances."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An ALB listener rule can forward to several target groups with weights. Changing the weights moves traffic between versions within seconds, with no DNS caching delays, and setting the old group back to 100% rolls back instantly.",
      "Weighted DNS records can split traffic, but the scenario rules out DNS changes, and clients that cache DNS make shifts and rollbacks slow.",
      "Mixing both versions in one target group gives no control over the share of traffic and no instant rollback.",
      "An instance refresh replaces instances in one group. Rolling back means another refresh, which is not instant."] },

{ id: "d3-60", d: 3,
  q: "A CloudOps engineer deletes a CloudFormation stack for a retired application. The deletion fails with DELETE_FAILED because the stack's S3 bucket is not empty. The data in the bucket is no longer needed.\n\nWhat should the engineer do?",
  o: ["Empty the bucket, and then delete the stack again.",
      "Turn on termination protection, and delete again.",
      "Add a DeletionPolicy of Delete to the bucket only.",
      "Run drift detection, and then execute a change set."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudFormation cannot delete an S3 bucket that still contains objects. After the objects, and all object versions if versioning is on, are deleted, the stack deletion can finish. If the data were still needed, the bucket could be retained when the stack is deleted.",
      "Termination protection prevents a stack from being deleted, which is the opposite of what is needed.",
      "Delete is already the default policy, and it still cannot delete a bucket that has objects in it.",
      "Drift detection and change sets do not remove objects from the bucket."] },

{ id: "d3-61", d: 3,
  q: "A company uses AWS CodeDeploy in-place deployments to release new versions of an application to 10 Amazon EC2 instances. At least 9 instances must stay in service at all times during a deployment.\n\nWhich deployment configuration should a CloudOps engineer choose?",
  o: ["CodeDeployDefault.OneAtATime",
      "CodeDeployDefault.HalfAtATime",
      "CodeDeployDefault.AllAtOnce",
      "A custom configuration with 50% minimum healthy hosts"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["OneAtATime deploys to one instance at a time, so 9 of the 10 instances stay in service throughout the deployment.",
      "HalfAtATime deploys to up to half of the instances at once, which leaves only 5 in service.",
      "AllAtOnce deploys to every instance at the same time, which can take the whole application out of service.",
      "A 50% minimum healthy hosts setting allows 5 instances to be out of service at once."] },

{ id: "d3-62", d: 3,
  q: "A security vendor provides an agent as installer packages for Windows and Linux. A CloudOps engineer must install the agent on 1,000 managed instances and update it to new versions later. The right package must be chosen automatically for each operating system.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Create a Systems Manager Distributor package, and install it with a State Manager association.",
      "Copy the installers to every instance with a script, and run them by hand during a maintenance window.",
      "Bake the agent into new AMIs for both operating systems, and replace instances when the agent changes.",
      "Store both installers in an S3 bucket, and ask each application team to install the right one."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Distributor packages software for multiple platforms and architectures and installs the right version on each managed node. Installing the package through State Manager keeps it installed, and moving to a new version is a single change.",
      "Manual installation across 1,000 instances is slow and error prone, and every update repeats the same work.",
      "Replacing instances for every agent update is disruptive and much more work.",
      "Relying on each team is inconsistent and gives no central view of what is installed."] },

{ id: "d3-63", d: 3,
  q: "A script refreshes currency exchange rates from a partner API and writes them to a DynamoDB table every 15 minutes. The script runs as a cron job on a dedicated t3.micro instance that must be patched and sometimes stops. The script finishes in under 1 minute.\n\nWhich solution will run the script with the LEAST operational overhead?",
  o: ["Move the script to a Lambda function, and invoke it every 15 minutes with EventBridge Scheduler.",
      "Move the cron job to a larger instance, and add an Auto Scaling group to keep one instance running.",
      "Move the script into an ECS service that runs one task all the time and sleeps 15 minutes in a loop.",
      "Turn on DynamoDB Streams, and run the script whenever an item in the table is written or changed."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Lambda runs short tasks with no servers to patch, and EventBridge Scheduler invokes it at a fixed rate with retries. A task that finishes in under 1 minute fits well within the 15-minute Lambda limit.",
      "This keeps a server to patch and adds cost, which is the overhead the team wants to remove.",
      "A task that runs all the time and sleeps wastes resources and still needs its own scheduling logic.",
      "Streams trigger on table changes, not on a schedule, so the rates would not be refreshed every 15 minutes."] },

{ id: "d3-64", d: 3,
  q: "A team stores its Terraform code in Git. The team wants every proposed change to be reviewed with the exact infrastructure changes before it is applied. The team also wants to find, each day, any manual changes that were made outside Terraform.\n\nWhich combination of practices will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Run terraform plan in the pull request pipeline, and require review of the plan before merging.",
      "Run terraform plan on a daily schedule, and alert when it shows changes that nobody proposed.",
      "Run terraform apply from each engineer's laptop after a peer looks at the code diff.",
      "Run terraform destroy and then apply each night so that the environment matches the code.",
      "Turn on CloudTrail Insights so that Terraform detects manual changes the next time it runs."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["terraform plan shows exactly what will be created, changed, or destroyed. Running it in the pull request and requiring review before merging lets reviewers see the infrastructure impact, not just the code diff.",
      "A scheduled plan compares the real infrastructure with the code and state. Unexpected differences show drift from manual changes, so the team can fix them quickly.",
      "Applying from laptops skips a consistent, reviewed plan and makes it hard to control who changes what.",
      "Destroying and re-creating the environment every night causes outages and data loss.",
      "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API call rates. It does not tell Terraform about drift."] },

{ id: "d3-65", d: 3,
  q: "S3 events invoke a Lambda function asynchronously. When the function still fails after its retries, the event is lost. The team wants failed events sent to an SQS queue along with error details, and successful results sent to an EventBridge event bus.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Configure Lambda destinations: on failure to the SQS queue, and on success to the event bus.",
      "Add a try and catch block to the function code that writes every event to both targets before returning.",
      "Set the function's reserved concurrency to 1 so that events are processed one at a time without failures.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning so that failed events can be replayed from the previous versions of the objects."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["For asynchronous invocations, Lambda destinations send a record of each invocation to a target based on the result. The on-failure record includes the original event and error details, and on-success records can go to an EventBridge event bus, with no code changes.",
      "Code inside the function cannot capture failures such as timeouts that stop the function, and it adds logic to maintain.",
      "Lower concurrency does not prevent failures, and it slows processing.",
      "Object versions are not events, and versioning does not keep a record of failed invocations."] },
]);
